         "Congress supporters celebrate retaining control of Delhi The Indian media have praised voters for refusing to allow the attacks in Mumbai to dominate the recent elections in six key states. Livelihood issues on a local level were more of a concern for voters and the main opposition BJP was criticised for playing the \"terror card\". The ruling Congress party won three states, the BJP two, while voting in Jammu and Kashmir is ongoing. India must hold a general election before May next year. 'Sigh of relief' Writing for the BBC, leading political analyst Mahesh Rangarajan said the \"Mumbai attacks became an 11th-hour poll issue, but the campaign fell flat\". \"Terror as an issue did not polarise voters on religious lines; in fact the bid to politicise the subject did not yield a political benefit.\" See a map of states voting With all the votes counted, Congress won Delhi by 42 seats to the Bharatiya Janata Party's 23 and captured Rajasthan, winning 96 seats to the BJP's 78. In Mizoram in the north-east, Congress swept back to power for the first time in a decade. It won 32 of the 40 seats. ELECTION TIMETABLE 14 and 20 November: Chattisgarh 27 November:"
